  "id": "COMPOUND:244 - PROTEIN:1380",
  "verbalization": "Doxycycline is implicated in the regulation of ATP synthase subunit a through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that doxycycline acts as a target for multiple compounds, including 72 kDa type IV collagenase, N-[(3-hydroxy-4-methoxyphenyl)methyl]-7-methyloctanamide, and 2,3-dihydroxybenzoic acid, all of which are themselves targets of ATP synthase subunit a. This indicates that doxycycline may exert its effects through a common pathway, where it indirectly influences the activity of ATP synthase subunit a via these intermediates. The presence of multiple targets suggests a potential role for doxycycline in modulating metabolic processes, possibly by interacting with enzymes involved in energy production. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship, where doxycycline may act as a regulator of metabolic pathways through a network of molecular interactions that ultimately affect ATP synthase subunit a.",
